A||||3||Bozone||What's with 86GT temp gauges?
Z||000000||Bozone||04-11-99||11:42 AM||||Is it just me or does anyone else out there in Fieroland have an 86Gt with bogus engine temp gauges? I have 2 GTs: one has its needle point north all the time, hot or cold; the other one, my dialy driver just had the needle fall right off the pin and is laying on the floor of the gauge glass. Has anyone fixed one of these? It looks like it might be easier to hook up a Steward-Warner newie than fix the original one. Any ideas?
Z||000001||PaUL||04-12-99||08:21 PM||||hey Bozo or Bozone is it, I did just that, made my own dash with all new gauges. I had to use an electrically driven speedo from VDO but the rest are conventional aftermarket gauges. 
Z||000002||bonnovy||04-15-99||07:52 PM||||I had the same problem with my '86SE. I had to remove the pod( good luck as it is a real pain) and then let the car idle for about 15 minutes (it was mid summer also) and then "slooowly" opened the coolant filler cap in the engine compartment and checked to see that the thermostat was open. I took a good guess that the engine temp was around 185-190 deg , and gently pushed the needle back on the post to the corrosponding number. I know that this is only close but I can tell if the thing is getting unusually hot now. Again use caution when opening the filler cap with the engine warm. It has been working for about 6 months now. It only cost some time and still is original equiptment.
Z||000003||Bozone||04-21-99||04:12 PM||||My problem could be solved with a simple storebought gauge, I guess. But I want at least one of these GTs to be classic stock so I guess it means tearing apart the pod. Soooo i guess it means doing one gets doing the both of them - second one's a piece of cake, right? But where did Pontiac get off... putting such junky gauges in a car they made? Who ever heard of the needles falling off? Jeez!!
